{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1001", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "Hark! A most fair journey dost thou seek to Japan’s fair shores? Then lend thine ear to this most cunning of itineraries wrought in the tongue of Master Shakespeare.\n\n**Act I: Scene I – Of Tokyo’s Grandeur**\n\nUpon the morrow thou shalt awaken in fair Tokyo. First let thy feet bear thee to the grand palace of the Shogun’s heir where the East doth meet the West in splendid discord. Behold the Imperial Palace and its gardens so fair they might make Venus weep. Then hasten to the bustling thoroughfare of Shibuya where youth in multitudes doth flock like starlings at eventide. There shalt thou dine upon sushi so fresh it seemeth the fish still leapt in the sea ere thy plate it graced.\n\n**Act I: Scene II – Of Nikko’s Sacred Splendor**\n\nUpon the second day thou shalt journey to Nikko’s hallowed glades. There lieth the Toshogu Shrine adorned with carvings so fine they could move the very stones to tears. Walk upon the sacred bridge and ponder the fleeting nature of man as did the poets of old. In the eve thou shalt rest in a ryokan most humble where the waters of an onsen shall cleanse thy soul as did the purging fires of Purgatory.\n\n**Act II: Scene I – Of Kyoto’s Imperial Past**\n\nThe third morn shall see thee in Kyoto the ancient seat of emperors. Visit the Kinkaku-ji its golden pavilion gleaming like the sun caught in a spider’s web. Then to the Arashiyama’s bamboo grove where the stalks whisper secrets older than time itself. In the evening thou shalt witness a tea ceremony most reverent where the matcha’s bitterness doth cleanse the palate of all earthly cares.\n\n**Act II: Scene II – Of Nara’s Gentle Beasts**\n\nUpon the fourth day thou shalt fare to Nara where the deer roam as tame as courtiers in the king’s own court. Bow thine head to the great Buddha of Todai-ji and marvel at its size which could swallow all thy doubts whole. Walk the paths of Nara Park and let the deer’s gentle gaze remind thee of man’s lost innocence.\n\n**Act III: Scene I – Of Osaka’s Merry Feast**\n\nThe fifth day shall bring thee to Osaka a city of mirth and merriment. Scale the heights of Osaka Castle and survey thy kingdom as did Henry atop his throne. In the evening make haste to Dotonbori where the lights dance upon the water like fireflies in a moonlit glade. There sup upon takoyaki and okonomiyaki and let thy heart be merry as the fools in a Shakespearean revel.\n\n**Act III: Scene II – Of Hiroshima’s Solemn Memory**\n\nUpon the sixth morn thou shalt journey to Hiroshima the city reborn from ashes like the phoenix of old. Visit the Peace Memorial Park and let the cenotaph’s silence speak to thee of war’s cruel hand. In the eve thou shalt dine upon okonomiyaki once more and ponder the fleeting nature of peace.\n\n**Epilogue: Scene I – Of Miyajima’s Floating Splendor**\n\nThe seventh and final day shall see thee upon the sacred isle of Miyajima. There stand the grand torii gate of Itsukushima as though it floated upon the waves themselves. Walk the shrine’s paths and let the tide’s ebb and flow remind thee of time’s relentless march. Then in the soft light of eventide thou shalt take thy leave of this fair land with a heart full and a mind enriched by its wonders.\n\nThus endeth thy journey in Japan’s fair realm. Go forth with joy and let the memories thereof be as a sweet sonnet in thy mind’s eye. Farewell!", "logprobs": [{"token": "H", "logprob": -2.974317789077759}, {"token": "ark", "logprob": -0.0013690156629309058}, {"token": "!", "logprob": -0.05635247379541397}, {"token": " A", "logprob": -1.2582082748413086}, {"token": " most", "logprob": -1.1750731468200684}, {"token": " fair", "logprob": -3.1760826110839844}, {"token": " journey", "logprob": -1.5492733716964722}, {"token": " dost", "logprob": -1.4846445322036743}, {"token": " thou", "logprob": -0.02047252655029297}, {"token": " seek", "logprob": -0.487639